      "writtenOn": "2014-12-03T15:20:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m confused. getDefaultProxy() returns the proxy of the default network, right? Why are we setting our proxy to that?\n\nShouldn\u0027t we set the proxy specified by the network\u0027s LinkProperties?",
      "revId": "8fbcb6be0d56ab0fd2c0872bca0628d3ad100577",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bf3dc54e_096d31f6",
        "filename": "core/java/android/net/ConnectivityManager.java",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 2518,
      "author": {
        "id": 1016583
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-12-05T03:27:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "getDefaultProxy() returns the proxy that best applies to this app.  If there is a global proxy, this is used, otherwise if the process is bound to a network (likely at this callsite), the network\u0027s proxy is used, otherwise the default network\u0027s proxy is used (also likely at this callsite if netId\u003d\u003dNETID_UNSET).  getDefaultProxy() returns to us exactly what we want to update the system properties to.",
      "parentUuid": "9fb3a1f9_2b90f1b1",
      "revId": "8fbcb6be0d56ab0fd2c0872bca0628d3ad100577",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9fb3a1f9_6b9ae9d1",
        "filename": "core/java/android/net/Network.java",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 248,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-12-03T15:20:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think we should honour or set the global proxy here at all. This method is completely bound to a given Network, and its documentation explicitly promises that it will only send traffic on the Network. But the global proxy certainly won\u0027t know that it needs to send all traffic on that Network, and in fact it might not even be reachable from that Network at all.\n\nFor example, consider an app that needs to send an MMS on a network that can only reach the MMS server but not the Internet. Such an app would not be able to use this method.",
      "revId": "8fbcb6be0d56ab0fd2c0872bca0628d3ad100577",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5f19292f_75bea8a5",
        "filename": "core/java/android/net/Network.java",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 248,
      "author": {
        "id": 1016583
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-12-05T03:27:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As we discussed extensively in the Monday night meeting, the global proxy for better or worse is designed to apply to all networks, all the time.  A global proxy is only used in fairly extreme cases to truly lock down a device.  If someone installs a global proxy to, say, protect a child from adult content, I would imagine blocking MMS is a first step :)\n\nAnother way to look at this is, we\u0027re carrying over the KK behavior.\n\nIf an app wants to bypass the proxy there are many ways to do so, e.g. the openConnection(URL, Proxy) method I\u0027m adding, by bundling their own HTTP stack, or with setProcessDefaultNetwork() and URL.openConnection(Proxy).",
      "parentUuid": "9fb3a1f9_6b9ae9d1",
      "revId": "8fbcb6be0d56ab0fd2c0872bca0628d3ad100577",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
